From: Muhammad Huda Nugroho <mhudan21@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] PCI: qcom: do not release a PHY whose power-on failed

qcom_pcie_phy_power_on() unwinds a failed phy_power_on() by calling
qcom_pcie_phy_power_off(), which walks the whole port list - including the port
that just failed, and every port the loop has not reached yet.

phy_power_off() decrements phy->power_count unconditionally and only invokes the
provider's ->power_off at a count of 1, while phy_power_on() only invokes
->power_on at a count of 0. Releasing a reference that was never acquired
therefore drives the count to -1, and the next phy_power_on() quietly skips
powering the PHY up: no error, no message, just a PHY that is never initialised
again. The PHY core already unwinds the failed acquisition itself, so this
release is both wrong and unnecessary.

On a single-port controller the very first failure is enough to reach -1. It is
normally invisible because a failed host init at probe time is fatal anyway, but
it becomes reachable the moment anything retries host init at runtime - as
system resume does, and as the link recovery in the next patch does.

Unwind with list_for_each_entry_continue_reverse() so only the ports that were
successfully powered on are powered off, and fail the phy_set_mode_ext() case
the same way instead of leaking the ports already acquired.

Signed-off-by: Muhammad Huda Nugroho <mhudan21@gmail.com>
---
--- a/drivers/pci/controller/dwc/pcie-qcom.c
+++ b/drivers/pci/controller/dwc/pcie-qcom.c
@@ -1262,16 +1262,29 @@
 	list_for_each_entry(port, &pcie->ports, list) {
 		ret = phy_set_mode_ext(port->phy, PHY_MODE_PCIE, PHY_MODE_PCIE_RC);
 		if (ret)
-			return ret;
+			goto err_power_off;
 
 		ret = phy_power_on(port->phy);
-		if (ret) {
-			qcom_pcie_phy_power_off(pcie);
-			return ret;
-		}
+		if (ret)
+			goto err_power_off;
 	}
 
 	return 0;
+
+err_power_off:
+	/*
+	 * Only release what was actually acquired. phy_power_off() decrements
+	 * phy->power_count unconditionally and invokes the provider's callback
+	 * only at 1, while phy_power_on() invokes it only at 0, so releasing a
+	 * port whose phy_power_on() failed (or was never reached) drives the
+	 * count negative - after which the next phy_power_on() silently skips
+	 * powering the PHY up at all. The PHY core already unwinds the failed
+	 * acquisition itself.
+	 */
+	list_for_each_entry_continue_reverse(port, &pcie->ports, list)
+		phy_power_off(port->phy);
+
+	return ret;
 }
 
 static int qcom_pcie_host_init(struct dw_pcie_rp *pp)
